WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.360
On mid-range Android's running React Native 0.76, an 800 millisecond cold start is standard,

2
00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:11.360
while 500 milliseconds represents the high-performance benchmark.

3
00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:15.120
This timeline illustrates the window available for optimization.

4
00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:20.320
Within this sub-second span, every millisecond of execution must be accounted for.

5
00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:25.920
A common starting point is placing a console.log timestamp at the top of the JavaScript bundle.

6
00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:28.640
This approach creates a significant blind spot.

7
00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:33.280
The JS thread doesn't execute until native OS initialization finishes.

8
00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:36.240
Measuring here ignores most of the launch sequence.

9
00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:40.400
Accurate diagnostics require measuring application startup at the native level.

10
00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:42.720
On Android, use the Perfetto UI.

11
00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:48.640
On iOS, use the Instruments template, with the RCT profile equals one flag active.

12
00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:51.360
Treating startup as pure JavaScript execution,

13
00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:54.080
obscures the crucial native latency events.

14
00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:56.560
Track performance at the C++ level.

15
00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:01.920
Opening a cold start trace in Perfetto provides a high-resolution view of the initialization sequence.

16
00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:06.240
Looking at the early moments of the trace, the JavaScript thread lane is empty.

17
00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:08.960
The work happening here is entirely native.

18
00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:12.880
The sequence begins with phase one, React host start.

19
00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:16.800
During this slice, the system performs C++ initialization

20
00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:19.680
before the JavaScript bundle is even accessed.

21
00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:23.040
Phase two follows, with Hermes executor.load bundle,

22
00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:26.160
where the operating system memory maps the bundle.

23
00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:29.120
Phase three, labeled runtime.call function,

24
00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:33.040
represents the execution of app registry.run application.

25
00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:38.800
The sequence concludes with phase four, fabric.comit, the first UI render pass.

26
00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:43.840
These four sequential native slices define the architectural limits of the startup metric.

27
00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:49.120
Optimization effort is only effective when it targets one of these specific native slices.

28
00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:52.480
Shrinking phase one involves eager React host warmup,

29
00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:57.520
which moves the C++ initialization to an earlier point in the application lifecycle.

30
00:01:57.520 --> 00:01:59.920
This introduces a memory trade-off.

31
00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:06.320
Eager warmup consumes RAM regardless of what a user eventually navigates to a React native screen.

32
00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:12.080
This strategy is typically reserved for apps where analytics show at least 30 to 40% of users

33
00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:14.640
consistently reach an RN surface.

34
00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:18.720
For phase two, inline requires is the primary intervention.

35
00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:23.440
Instead of executing every module's top-level code during bundle evaluation,

36
00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:26.960
inline requires that work until the module is actually called.

37
00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:31.840
The impact can be verified by running the React Native bundle command with the profile flag

38
00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:34.560
and measuring the change in total load time.

39
00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:38.800
Phase three is optimized by moving non-critical logic out of the top-level bundle

40
00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:42.320
using interactionmanager.run after interactions.

41
00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:46.720
This queues work until touch animations are complete and the first frame has been committed,

42
00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:49.040
preventing thread contention during launch.

43
00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:53.440
Implementing this can reduce the phase three execution path from 300 milliseconds

44
00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:55.600
to approximately 80 milliseconds.

45
00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:58.400
Phase four requires an audit of the initial render path

46
00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:02.160
to ensure only components required for the first frame are processed.

47
00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:06.800
Achieving a 500 millisecond cold start relies on systematically delaying code execution

48
00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:09.360
away from these four critical native paths.

49
00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:14.160
Beyond the initial render, runtime performance is governed by the Hermes engine's execution model.

50
00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:18.320
Hermes utilizes ahead of time execution rather than adjust in time compiler.

51
00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:20.720
While jick compilers can achieve higher peak throughput,

52
00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:23.680
they introduce unpredictable latency spikes during warm-up.

53
00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:27.760
Hermes trades that peak speed for a consistent predictable latency profile.

54
00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:31.680
This predictability is the priority for mobile UIs where maintaining a steady frame rate

55
00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:34.320
is more important than raw numeric processing power.

56
00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:38.160
Severe performance degradation in Hermes typically stems from garbage collection pressure

57
00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:40.240
rather than hitting a raw processor ceiling.

58
00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:42.640
Hermes uses a generational garbage collector.

59
00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:45.760
Most objects are handled in the young generation heap,

60
00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:48.480
while long-lived objects move to the old generation.

61
00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:53.200
Minor GCs in the young generation are frequent but fast,

62
00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:56.000
usually causing pauses of two to five milliseconds.

63
00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:00.000
Major GCs in the old generation are more intensive,

64
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:03.760
potentially causing UI freezes between 10 and 50 milliseconds.

65
00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:09.040
These events appear in Perfetto as GC slices on the JS thread.

66
00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:13.520
If they overlap with UI animations, the app has an allocation problem.

67
00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:17.840
To find the source, the engine's heap timeline can be loaded into chrome dev tools

68
00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:21.920
to identify which constructors are responsible for the largest retained memory size.

69
00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:28.400
CPU diagnostics often reveal that wide bars in a flame chart aren't caused by slow JS logic,

70
00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:33.360
but by excessive native bridge serialization or unnecessary derived state calculations.

71
00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:38.880
Optimizing Hermes' performance requires managing memory allocation and serialization costs.

72
00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:45.040
Rather than focusing on CPU cycles, maintaining a locked 120 frames per second

73
00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:48.800
during complex gestures presents a specific challenge.

74
00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:51.360
The saturation of the main JavaScript thread.

75
00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:56.000
Standard gesture callbacks must cross the bridge to the JS thread

76
00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:58.720
before a UI update can be triggered.

77
00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:02.320
This introduces a mandatory one to two frame latency.

78
00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:07.440
Reanimated three workloads are designed to bypass this bridge entirely.

79
00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:13.840
The Worklet directive signals the compiler to run the function inside a secondary Hermes runtime,

80
00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:16.000
initialized directly on the UI thread.

81
00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:18.640
This secondary runtime is isolated.

82
00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:22.560
Worklets cannot access closures from the main JavaScript thread.

83
00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:27.120
Communication between these threads must be handled through shared values,

84
00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:29.360
which provide a synchronized bridge for state.

85
00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:33.680
By detaching the animation loop from the main JavaScript thread,

86
00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:38.880
gestures can remain responsive even when the main thread is occupied with background tasks.

87
00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:43.440
An optimized react native app moves through a condensed native cold start,

88
00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:47.600
maintains predictable latency and isolates gestures on the UI thread.

89
00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:50.880
Native tracking, generational memory management,

90
00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:54.320
and thread isolation are the pillars of production grade performance.

91
00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:58.720
Standard web tools offer little visibility into the native bottlenecks,

92
00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:00.640
defining the react native lifecycle.

93
00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:05.920
High performance react native engineering is a discipline focused on hardware constraints,

94
00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:10.960
requiring an understanding of the C++ and native threading architecture beneath the code.

